      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "STUDY DESIGN",
          "content": "Prospective, randomized, double-blind clinical study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To examine the effect of meperidine-impregnated autogenous free fat grafts (AFFGs) on postoperative pain management after 1-level, first-time lumbar disc surgery."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "SUMMARY OF BACKGROUND DATA",
          "content": "Uncontrolled postoperative pain impedes patient recovery. Insufficient control of postoperative pain makes it difficult to convince new patients to undergo the lumbar disc surgery."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Twenty-seven patients scheduled to undergo lumbar microdiscectomy for the first time were divided into 2 groups by a randomized double-blind method. Group 1 patients received a meperidine-impregnated AFFG in the epidural area. Group 2 received the graft without meperidine impregnation. Both groups were asked to use a Patient Controlled Analgesia device to deliver tramadol, and at 1, 4, 12, and 24 hours postoperatively, the amounts of tramadol used and pain severity on the 10-cm Visual Analog Scale (VAS) were recorded."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The VAS scores and total tramadol use were both lower in group 1 compared with the control group (P<0.05). The first hour of VAS scores were significantly lower in group 1 than in the control group (P<0.05)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "In this study, we helped patients, who underwent 1-level, first-time lumbar microdiscectomy have a postoperative pain-free and comfortable period by using epidural meperidine-impregnated AFFGs."
        }
      ]},
